00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:53.760
for a living. So my parents were

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:54.780
interested in the news.

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:56.310
I heard about it at home.

00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:58.740
We weren't a lot like a lot of families

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:00.720
where they don't they didn't pay

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:02.190
attention. They don't know and give a

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:03.690
shit. They don't give a shit.

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:05.580
Politics just wasn't what was talked

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:08.430
about. So it was always in my ear.

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:10.800
And my father was a good living room

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:12.120
comedian, which is something a lot of

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:14.310
comedians will tell you that it kind of

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:16.320
gestates over one generation that the

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:18.660
the parent one or maybe both parents.

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:21.930
My mother was witty to show you that in

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:24.150
the home again the the background of

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:27.030
what you have at home and and so like

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:30.420
the idea that I would go into comedy

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:33.600
with a bent toward not just news but

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:35.490
stuff that was in the culture,

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:37.650
something that was meaty and important

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:39.540
and something you could talk about and

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:42.150
chew on that has some intellectual

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:43.950
